Chapter: KeyInput
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 One keyboard read, translated once. `uefi-read-key` returns the raw
 PS/2 Set-1 make code the IRQ1 handler left in the key cell (0 when no
 key is waiting). A make code is a key POSITION: the hardware has no
 opinion about characters and performs no encoding of any kind. `poll-key`
 turns that position into a canonical key identity so every app agrees on
 what a key value means:

   - a character is its **CCE code point**, which is the encoding
     everything inside Codex uses. Compare against a character literal:
     `k == char-code 'q'`. Do not write a number.
   - a letter is cased by shift XOR caps, so text entry is faithful
   - every key that is not a character is a **named key**, a value at or
     above `key-named-base` with a name in this chapter: `key-enter`,
     `key-backspace`, `key-tab`, `key-escape`, `key-delete`, the four
     arrows, `key-home` / `key-end` / `key-pgup` / `key-pgdn`, and
     `key-f1` through `key-f12`
   - the shift, ctrl and alt state at the time of the key is read with
     `poll-mods` (shift 1, ctrl 2, alt 4), for chords like Ctrl+S

 `0` means no key, which is the idle value every event loop already
 tests.

 **This chapter used to emit ASCII, and two things were wrong with that.**

 The first was the encoding. The keyboard is an input boundary, and rule 5
 of this project says conversion happens at boundaries and everything
 inside is CCE. This chapter converted the wrong way: it turned a scancode
 into ASCII and handed that to application code, which is inside. Two
 character alphabets then circulated at once, kept apart by warnings in
 prose rather than by anything a compiler could check, and the warnings
 were real: `key-upper` carried one, `apps/browser` had CCE tokenizer
 values sitting beside ASCII key values, and every chapter crossing the
 seam wrote its own conversion. There is one alphabet now.

 The second was that characters and named keys shared one integer space.
 The named keys were DOM key codes, and DOM key codes land on printable
 characters: **Up was 38 and so was shift+7, Right was 39 and so was the
 apostrophe**, F1 through F10 were 112-121 which are the letters `p`
 through `y`, and F5 was 116 which is `t`. Measured, not reasoned: a
 `-keys-file` timeline typing shift+7, Up, apostrophe and Right returned
 38, 38, 39, 39. The old contract stated both halves of that collision on
 adjacent lines of this very prose and nobody noticed, because no test
 typed a character and its colliding named key in the same timeline.
 `codex/test/keys-collision` is that test now.

 A named key is deliberately outside anything CCE can represent, so if one
 ever leaks into text it renders as the replacement character rather than
 as a plausible wrong letter. `codex/test/keys-collision` pins that too.

 The modifiers are decoded through a latch, because a keyboard hands you a
 modifier and the key as two separate events and the cell only ever holds
 one code at a time. `uefi-read-key` returns make codes AND break codes --
 a break code is the make code with bit 7 set, which the read preserves (it
 masks to a full byte, not to seven bits) -- so poll-key can watch a
 modifier go down and come back up. `kbd-mod-addr` is a bitmask: bit 0
 shift, bit 1 ctrl, bit 2 alt, bit 3 caps. Shift (make 42/54, break
 170/182), ctrl (make 29, break 157) and alt (make 56, break 184) are HELD
 -- the make sets the bit, the break clears it. Caps Lock (make 58) is a
 TOGGLE -- its make flips bit 3 and its break (186) is ignored. Left and
 right modifiers share a bit; the right-hand ones arrive as `0xE0` (224)
 then the same make code, and since 224 translates to nothing the bare code
 behind it sets the same bit, so RCtrl and RAlt work without a second case.

 A letter comes back cased by **shift XOR caps** -- lowercase (97-122) when
 exactly one of them is active, uppercase (65-90) when both or neither --
 and the shifted number row and punctuation come back as their shifted
 symbols. This is a change of contract from the pre-shift world: a letter
 used to be its uppercase IDENTITY regardless of shift, so a call site that
 reads a letter as a command shortcut (`== 81` for Q) needs `key-upper`
 (below). Text entry is the winner: a passphrase read through poll-key is
 case-sensitive, which is why every identity provisioned before the shift
 change stops unlocking (the migration was chosen deliberately).

 `poll-mods` reports the held modifiers -- shift 1, ctrl 2, alt 4, the same
 bit values `UI chapter Event` uses (`mod-shift`, `mod-ctrl`, `mod-alt`) --
 so an event loop can stamp them onto the `EvKeyDown` it builds and a
 handler can tell Ctrl+S from a bare S. Caps is deliberately absent from it:
 it is already folded into the letter's case, and it is a lock rather than a
 chord.

 The numpad and the dedicated arrow beside it share a make code, and two
 more latch bits tell them apart. NumLock (make 69, break 197 ignored) is a
 TOGGLE like caps: its make flips bit 4. A dedicated nav key arrives as
 `0xE0` (224) then the make code, so 224 sets a one-shot bit 5 that the very
 next key consumes: a `0xE0`-prefixed key is always its dedicated meaning
 (an arrow, NumLock-independent), while a BARE numpad key is its digit when
 NumLock is on (`0x48` -> `8`) and its arrow when off (`0x48` -> Up). The
 numpad `-`, `+` and `*` are their symbols in either state, and numpad `/`
 and Enter arrive `0xE0`-prefixed. NumLock and the one-shot are internal to
 the latch: `poll-mods` still masks to the shift/ctrl/alt three.

  kbd-mod-addr : Integer = 36752

 We say:

Section: Named Keys

 A named key is not a character and must never be confused with one, so it
 lives above every value CCE can produce. CCE is allocated densely from
 zero: tier 0 fills 0-127, tier 1 runs to 2175, and tier 2's ten blocks sum
 to 41060 more, so 43235 is the highest code point that exists today and
 `to-unicode` answers the replacement character above it. `key-named-base`
 sits far enough above that band that CCE growth cannot reach it: CCE is a
 compaction of Unicode's assigned code points, of which there are roughly
 150000, so a base of 2^20 has an order of magnitude of headroom.

 The margin is a claim, so it is tested rather than asserted:
 `codex/test/keys-collision` requires `to-unicode key-f1` to answer the
 replacement character, which fails the day the two spaces meet.

  key-named-base : Integer = 1048576

  key-is-named : Integer -> Boolean
  key-is-named (k) = k >= key-named-base

  key-enter : Integer = 1048577
  key-backspace : Integer = 1048578
  key-tab : Integer = 1048579
  key-escape : Integer = 1048580
  key-delete : Integer = 1048581

  key-left : Integer = 1048583
  key-up : Integer = 1048584
  key-right : Integer = 1048585
  key-down : Integer = 1048586
  key-home : Integer = 1048587
  key-end : Integer = 1048588
  key-pgup : Integer = 1048589
  key-pgdn : Integer = 1048590

  key-f1 : Integer = 1048597
  key-f2 : Integer = 1048598
  key-f3 : Integer = 1048599
  key-f4 : Integer = 1048600
  key-f5 : Integer = 1048601
  key-f6 : Integer = 1048602
  key-f7 : Integer = 1048603
  key-f8 : Integer = 1048604
  key-f9 : Integer = 1048605
  key-f10 : Integer = 1048606
  key-f11 : Integer = 1048607
  key-f12 : Integer = 1048608

Section: Poll

 Two sources, and the firmware one has to be asked first because on a large
 class of machines the other cannot answer at all. uefi-read-key reads the cell
 the IRQ1 handler fills from PS/2 port 0x60; a board with no PS/2 port -- which
 includes the ASUS TUF this project targets -- can never put anything in it, and
 no amount of USB work changes that. uefi-read-key-ex asks the firmware through
 EFI_SIMPLE_TEXT_INPUT_EX and works wherever boot services are live. It answers
 -1 when there is no key AND when there is no SystemTable, so a bare-metal boot
 with no firmware falls through to the cell for free.

 ScanCode and UnicodeChar are the two halves of EFI_INPUT_KEY and exactly one of
 them is meaningful per keystroke: a printable character arrives in UnicodeChar
 with ScanCode zero, and a function or arrow key the other way round.

 Cost worth knowing: uefi-read-key-ex performs a LocateProtocol on every call,
 so this is a slower poll than reading a cell. Correctness first; caching the
 interface is a later change and belongs in the helper, not here.

 The test is `== -1` and not `> 0` deliberately. -1 is the helper's documented
 and only failure value, for both "no key" and "no SystemTable", so testing it
 directly says exactly what is meant. A sign test additionally MEASURED WRONG
 on 2026-08-02: with the shift-state fold corrected, a probe confirmed the
 returned key has bit 63 clear and is neither 0 nor -1, which makes `k > 0`
 necessarily true, and `k > 0` still took the false branch across five runs on
 two independently built images. That is an open question about the comparison
 rather than about the keyboard, recorded in red-workplan; `== -1` is correct
 regardless of how it resolves.

  poll-key : Integer
  poll-key =
    let ek = uefi-read-key-ex
    in if ek == -1 then
      (let sc = uefi-read-key
       in poll-key-decode sc (atomic-load kbd-mod-addr))
    else efi-key-decode ek

  efi-key-decode : Integer -> Integer
  efi-key-decode (ek) =
    let ch = bit-and ek 65535
    in if ch == 0 then efi-scan-decode (bit-and (bit-shru ek 16) 65535)
    else if ch == 13 then key-enter
    else if ch == 8 then key-backspace
    else if ch == 9 then key-tab
    else ch

 EFI_INPUT_KEY.ScanCode values, UEFI spec table 12-3. They are not Set-1
 scancodes and share no numbering with poll-key-decode's input.

  efi-scan-decode : Integer -> Integer
  efi-scan-decode (sc) =
    if sc == 1 then key-up
    else if sc == 2 then key-down
    else if sc == 3 then key-right
    else if sc == 4 then key-left
    else if sc == 5 then key-home
    else if sc == 6 then key-end
    else if sc == 8 then key-delete
    else if sc == 9 then key-pgup
    else if sc == 10 then key-pgdn
    else if sc == 11 then key-f1
    else if sc == 12 then key-f2
    else if sc == 13 then key-f3
    else if sc == 14 then key-f4
    else if sc == 15 then key-f5
    else if sc == 16 then key-f6
    else if sc == 17 then key-f7
    else if sc == 18 then key-f8
    else if sc == 19 then key-f9
    else if sc == 20 then key-f10
    else if sc == 21 then key-f11
    else if sc == 22 then key-f12
    else if sc == 23 then key-escape
    else 0

  poll-key-decode : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  poll-key-decode (sc) (mods) =
    if sc == 0 then 0
    else if sc == 42 then mod-set mods 1
    else if sc == 54 then mod-set mods 1
    else if sc == 170 then mod-clear mods 1
    else if sc == 182 then mod-clear mods 1
    else if sc == 29 then mod-set mods 2
    else if sc == 157 then mod-clear mods 2
    else if sc == 56 then mod-set mods 4
    else if sc == 184 then mod-clear mods 4
    else if sc == 58 then let d = atomic-store kbd-mod-addr (bit-xor mods 8) in 0
    else if sc == 186 then 0
    else if sc == 69 then let d = atomic-store kbd-mod-addr (bit-xor mods 16) in 0
    else if sc == 197 then 0
    else if sc == 224 then mod-set mods 32
    else key-emit (decode-key sc mods)

 The translation tables below still work in ASCII, because ASCII is the
 alphabet a PS/2 layout is written in and rewriting a hundred table entries
 by hand is a way to introduce errors, not to remove them. `key-emit` is
 the single point where a character crosses into CCE, which is what makes
 this a boundary rather than a smear: one conversion, through
 `from-unicode`, which is the mapping the encoding chapter already tests.

 A named key passes through untouched, and so does the idle zero. Every
 printable ASCII character has a CCE code point (tier 0 carries exactly the
 thirty-two punctuation slots ASCII needs, which is not a coincidence), so
 the conversion cannot lose one; `codex/test/keys-collision` walks the
 printable range and requires every answer to be non-negative rather than
 taking that on trust.

  key-emit : Integer -> Integer
  key-emit (k) =
    if k <= 0 then 0
    else if k >= key-named-base then k
    else from-unicode k

  poll-mods : Integer
  poll-mods = bit-and (atomic-load kbd-mod-addr) 7

Section: Numpad

 `decode-key` runs for every key that is not a modifier. It consumes the
 `0xE0` one-shot (bit 5): a prefixed key is the dedicated arrow, decoded
 straight through `scancode-to-keycode` and independent of NumLock. A bare
 key routes through `numpad-or-default`: when NumLock (bit 4) is on and the
 code is a numpad digit, it becomes that digit; otherwise it falls through
 to the ordinary translation, which maps the bare numpad codes to the
 arrows exactly as before.

  decode-key : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  decode-key (sc) (mods) =
    let e0 = bit-and mods 32
    in let cleared = bit-xor mods (bit-and mods 32)
    in let d = atomic-store kbd-mod-addr cleared
    in if e0 > 0 then apply-mods (scancode-to-keycode sc) cleared
    else numpad-or-default sc cleared

  numpad-or-default : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  numpad-or-default (sc) (mods) =
    if bit-and mods 16 > 0
      then if is-numpad-digit sc then numpad-digit sc
        else apply-mods (scancode-to-keycode sc) mods
    else apply-mods (scancode-to-keycode sc) mods

  is-numpad-digit : Integer -> Boolean
  is-numpad-digit (sc) =
    if sc < 71 then False
    else if sc > 83 then False
    else if sc == 74 then False
    else if sc == 78 then False
    else True

  numpad-digit : Integer -> Integer
  numpad-digit (sc) =
    if sc == 71 then 55
    else if sc == 72 then 56
    else if sc == 73 then 57
    else if sc == 75 then 52
    else if sc == 76 then 53
    else if sc == 77 then 54
    else if sc == 79 then 49
    else if sc == 80 then 50
    else if sc == 81 then 51
    else if sc == 82 then 48
    else 46

Section: Modifiers

  mod-set : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mod-set (mods) (bit) =
    let d = atomic-store kbd-mod-addr (bit-or mods bit) in 0

  mod-clear : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  mod-clear (mods) (bit) =
    let d = atomic-store kbd-mod-addr (bit-xor mods (bit-and mods bit)) in 0

 `key-upper` folds a `poll-key` letter back to its uppercase command
 identity, for a call site that reads a letter as a keyboard SHORTCUT
 rather than as text: `key-upper k == char-code 'N'` fires on either case.

 It operates on CCE now, where lowercase is 13-38 and uppercase 39-64, so
 it is the same `+26` that `Foreword chapter CCE`'s own `to-upper` performs
 rather than a second implementation of casing in a different alphabet.
 That divergence was the thing the old warning here existed to manage, and
 the warning is gone because the divergence is. A named key is not a letter
 and passes through.

  key-upper : Integer -> Integer
  key-upper (k) =
    if k >= 13 then if k <= 38 then k + 26 else k
    else k

 A letter is cased by shift XOR caps; a non-letter takes its shifted symbol
 only when shift is held (caps does not shift punctuation). This still runs
 in ASCII, before `key-emit` converts, because the shifted-symbol table is
 a property of the keyboard layout rather than of the encoding. A named key
 takes neither: there is no shifted Up arrow.

  apply-mods : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  apply-mods (k) (mods) =
    if k >= key-named-base then k
    else if k >= 65 then if k <= 90 then apply-letter k mods else apply-symbol k mods
    else apply-symbol k mods

  apply-letter : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  apply-letter (k) (mods) =
    let shift = bit-and mods 1
    in let caps = bit-and mods 8
    in if shift > 0
      then if caps > 0 then k + 32 else k
      else if caps > 0 then k else k + 32

  apply-symbol : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  apply-symbol (k) (mods) =
    if bit-and mods 1 > 0 then shifted-symbol k else k

  shifted-symbol : Integer -> Integer
  shifted-symbol (k) =
    if k == 49 then 33
    else if k == 50 then 64
    else if k == 51 then 35
    else if k == 52 then 36
    else if k == 53 then 37
    else if k == 54 then 94
    else if k == 55 then 38
    else if k == 56 then 42
    else if k == 57 then 40
    else if k == 48 then 41
    else if k == 45 then 95
    else if k == 61 then 43
    else if k == 91 then 123
    else if k == 93 then 125
    else if k == 92 then 124
    else if k == 59 then 58
    else if k == 39 then 34
    else if k == 44 then 60
    else if k == 46 then 62
    else if k == 47 then 63
    else if k == 96 then 126
    else k

Section: Translation

  scancode-to-keycode : Integer -> Integer
  scancode-to-keycode (sc) =
    if sc == 0 then 0
    else if sc <= 15 then sk-low sc
    else if sc <= 41 then sk-row1 sc
    else if sc <= 57 then sk-row2 sc
    else sk-high sc

  sk-low : Integer -> Integer
  sk-low (sc) =
    if sc == 1 then key-escape
    else if sc == 14 then key-backspace
    else if sc == 15 then key-tab
    else if sc == 12 then 45
    else if sc == 13 then 61
    else if sc == 11 then 48
    else sc + 47

  sk-row1 : Integer -> Integer
  sk-row1 (sc) =
    if sc == 28 then key-enter
    else if sc == 26 then 91
    else if sc == 27 then 93
    else if sc == 29 then 0
    else if sc == 39 then 59
    else if sc == 40 then 39
    else if sc == 41 then 96
    else if sc <= 25 then sk-qwerty sc
    else sk-asdf sc

  sk-qwerty : Integer -> Integer
  sk-qwerty (sc) =
    if sc == 16 then 81
    else if sc == 17 then 87
    else if sc == 18 then 69
    else if sc == 19 then 82
    else if sc == 20 then 84
    else if sc == 21 then 89
    else if sc == 22 then 85
    else if sc == 23 then 73
    else if sc == 24 then 79
    else 80

  sk-asdf : Integer -> Integer
  sk-asdf (sc) =
    if sc == 30 then 65
    else if sc == 31 then 83
    else if sc == 32 then 68
    else if sc == 33 then 70
    else if sc == 34 then 71
    else if sc == 35 then 72
    else if sc == 36 then 74
    else if sc == 37 then 75
    else 76

  sk-row2 : Integer -> Integer
  sk-row2 (sc) =
    if sc == 57 then 32
    else if sc == 43 then 92
    else if sc == 51 then 44
    else if sc == 52 then 46
    else if sc == 53 then 47
    else if sc == 55 then 42
    else if sc == 42 then 0
    else if sc == 54 then 0
    else if sc == 56 then 0
    else sk-zxcv sc

  sk-zxcv : Integer -> Integer
  sk-zxcv (sc) =
    if sc == 44 then 90
    else if sc == 45 then 88
    else if sc == 46 then 67
    else if sc == 47 then 86
    else if sc == 48 then 66
    else if sc == 49 then 78
    else 77

 The navigation block and the function row are named keys. They used to be
 DOM key codes, which is where the collision with the character space came
 from: 36 is `$`, 38 is `&`, 39 is the apostrophe, and 112-123 are the
 letters `p` through `{`. The numpad `-` and `+` stay characters, because
 that is what they are.

  sk-high : Integer -> Integer
  sk-high (sc) =
    if sc == 71 then key-home
    else if sc == 72 then key-up
    else if sc == 73 then key-pgup
    else if sc == 75 then key-left
    else if sc == 77 then key-right
    else if sc == 79 then key-end
    else if sc == 80 then key-down
    else if sc == 81 then key-pgdn
    else if sc == 83 then key-delete
    else if sc == 87 then key-f11
    else if sc == 88 then key-f12
    else if sc == 74 then 45
    else if sc == 78 then 43
    else if sc >= 59 then sk-fkey sc
    else 0

  sk-fkey : Integer -> Integer
  sk-fkey (sc) = if sc <= 68 then key-f1 + (sc - 59) else 0
